Semi-supervised learning trains a model using a small \textbf{labeled} set \\
$L=\{(x_1,y_1),\ldots,(x_m,y_m)\}$ together with a much larger \textbf{unlabeled} set \\
$U=\{x_{m+1},\ldots,x_{m+n}\}$ whose true class $y$ is unknown. The goal is to use the pattern found in both sets together to build a better classifier than the labeled data alone could produce.

This sits exactly between the supervised setting of Chapter 2 (every example labeled) and the unsupervised setting of Chapter 3 (no example labeled).

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
\foreach \i/\c in {1/primaryblue,2/primaryblue,3/darkgray,4/accentorange,5/darkgray,6/darkgray,7/primaryblue,8/darkgray,9/accentorange,10/accentorange}
  \filldraw[\c] (\i*0.9,0) circle (4pt);
\node[font=\scriptsize,primaryblue] at (1.5,-0.6) {labeled A};
\node[font=\scriptsize,accentorange] at (8.1,-0.6) {labeled B};
\node[font=\scriptsize,darkgray] at (4.5,0.6) {unlabeled (class unknown)};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

A dataset has 100 emails: 15 are marked Spam or NotSpam by a human, and the remaining 85 have no label at all. Is this a semi-supervised learning problem?

\textbf{Solution:}
Labeled count $=15$, Unlabeled count $=85$. Since both a nonzero labeled set \textbf{and} a nonzero unlabeled set exist together in the same training data: \\
\boxed{\text{Yes, this is a semi-supervised learning problem}}

A dataset has 50 patient records, and every single record has a confirmed Disease/NoDisease label. Is this a semi-supervised learning problem?

\textbf{Solution:}
Labeled count $=50$, Unlabeled count $=0$. Since there is no unlabeled portion at all:
\boxed{\text{No, this is an ordinary (fully) supervised learning problem}}

A dataset has 200 customer purchase records, and none of them have any class label attached. Is this a semi-supervised learning problem?

\textbf{Solution:}
Labeled count $=0$, Unlabeled count $=200$. Since there is no labeled portion at all to anchor the classes:
\boxed{\text{No, this is an unsupervised learning problem (clustering, Chapter 3)}}

\textbf{Where Semi-Supervised Learning Fits:}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item \textbf{Supervised} (Chapter 2): $L$ only, $U=\varnothing$
\item \textbf{Unsupervised} (Chapter 3): $U$ only, $L=\varnothing$
\item \textbf{Semi-supervised} (this section): both $L\neq\varnothing$ and $U\neq\varnothing$ used together
\item Semi-supervised learning is valuable because labeling data (by a human) is expensive, while unlabeled data is often cheap and plentiful
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{The Expectation-Maximization Framework}

EM is an iterative procedure for estimating model parameters when some data is incomplete (here, the missing piece is the class label $y$ of the unlabeled examples). It alternates between two steps until the parameters stop changing:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item \textbf{E-step (Expectation):} using the current parameters, compute the probability of each possible class for every unlabeled example — a \textbf{soft} (fractional) label rather than a hard 0/1 label.
\item \textbf{M-step (Maximization):} using the labeled data together with these fractional soft labels, recompute the parameters that best fit all the data.
\end{itemize}

This is the same "guess, then refine, then guess again" idea as k-Means (Section 3.2): k-Means alternates Assign and Update; EM alternates E-step and M-step, except EM assigns \textit{fractional} membership instead of putting each point in exactly one cluster.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={rectangle,rounded corners,draw=primaryblue,thick,fill=primaryblue!8,align=center,font=\small,minimum width=2.6cm,minimum height=1cm},
  arr/.style={draw,thick,-{Stealth[length=2.5mm]}}
]
\node (init) at (0,3) {Initialize parameters\\ from labeled data $L$};
\node (e) at (-2.6,0) {E-step\\ soft-label $U$};
\node (m) at (2.6,0) {M-step\\ update parameters};
\draw[arr] (init) -- (e);
\draw[arr] (e) to[bend left=25] (m);
\draw[arr] (m) to[bend left=25] (e);
\node[draw=none,fill=none,font=\scriptsize] at (0,-1.4) {repeat until parameters stop changing};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item Each round of E-step followed by M-step never decreases how well the parameters fit the data \hfill\textit{(monotonic improvement)}
\item EM is guaranteed to converge, but only to a \textbf{local} optimum, not necessarily the best possible parameters \hfill\textit{(local convergence, same caution as k-Means)}
\item The E-step produces \textbf{soft} (fractional, e.g. 0.3/0.7) class probabilities for unlabeled points, not hard single-class labels \hfill\textit{(soft assignment)}
\item The initial parameters (estimated from $L$ alone) affect where EM converges \hfill\textit{(initialization sensitivity)}
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{EM for Semi-Supervised Naive Bayes Classification}

The labeled set $L$ below records Weather and whether cricket was played.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cc}
\toprule
Weather & PlayCricket \\
\midrule
Sunny & Yes \\
Sunny & Yes \\
Rainy & Yes \\
Rainy & No \\
Rainy & No \\
Sunny & No \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Compute the initial Naive Bayes parameters $P(\text{Yes})$, $P(\text{No})$, $P(\text{Sunny}\mid\text{Yes})$, $P(\text{Rainy}\mid\text{Yes})$, $P(\text{Sunny}\mid\text{No})$, $P(\text{Rainy}\mid\text{No})$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
P(\text{Yes}) &= \frac{3}{6} = 0.5, \qquad P(\text{No}) = \frac{3}{6} = 0.5 \\[4pt]
P(\text{Sunny}\mid\text{Yes}) &= \frac{2}{3} \approx 0.667, \qquad P(\text{Rainy}\mid\text{Yes}) = \frac{1}{3} \approx 0.333 \\
P(\text{Sunny}\mid\text{No}) &= \frac{1}{3} \approx 0.333, \qquad P(\text{Rainy}\mid\text{No}) = \frac{2}{3} \approx 0.667
\end{align*}
\boxed{\text{Initial parameters computed exactly as ordinary Naive Bayes on } L \text{ alone}}

An unlabeled example $U_1$ has Weather $=$ Rainy. Using the parameters from Example 4.4, compute the soft label $P(\text{Yes}\mid U_1)$ and $P(\text{No}\mid U_1)$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
\text{score}(\text{Yes}) &= P(\text{Yes})\cdot P(\text{Rainy}\mid\text{Yes}) = (0.5)(0.333) \approx 0.1667 \\
\text{score}(\text{No}) &= P(\text{No})\cdot P(\text{Rainy}\mid\text{No}) = (0.5)(0.667) \approx 0.3333 \\[4pt]
\text{Total} &= 0.1667+0.3333 = 0.5 \\[4pt]
P(\text{Yes}\mid U_1) &= \frac{0.1667}{0.5} \approx 0.333, \qquad P(\text{No}\mid U_1) = \frac{0.3333}{0.5} \approx 0.667
\end{align*}
\boxed{U_1 \text{ is soft-labeled } 33.3\%\text{ Yes and } 66.7\%\text{ No (not a hard 0/1 label)}}

Using the labeled set $L$ from Example 4.4 and the soft-labeled $U_1$ (Weather $=$ Rainy) from Example 4.5, recompute $P(\text{Yes})$, $P(\text{No})$, $P(\text{Rainy}\mid\text{Yes})$, and $P(\text{Rainy}\mid\text{No})$.

\textbf{Solution:}
$U_1$ contributes a fractional count of $0.333$ toward the Yes class and $0.667$ toward the No class.
\begin{align*}
\text{New Yes count} &= 3 + 0.333 = 3.333, \qquad \text{New No count} = 3 + 0.667 = 3.667 \\
\text{New total} &= 3.333+3.667 = 7 \\[4pt]
P(\text{Yes}) &= \frac{3.333}{7} \approx 0.476, \qquad P(\text{No}) = \frac{3.667}{7} \approx 0.524
\end{align*}
For the attribute likelihood, $U_1$'s Rainy value adds $0.333$ to the Yes-Rainy count and $0.667$ to the No-Rainy count:
\begin{align*}
\text{New Yes-Rainy count} &= 1+0.333 = 1.333, \qquad \text{New Yes total} = 3.333 \\
P(\text{Rainy}\mid\text{Yes}) &= \frac{1.333}{3.333} = 0.4 \\[6pt]
\text{New No-Rainy count} &= 2+0.667 = 2.667, \qquad \text{New No total} = 3.667 \\
P(\text{Rainy}\mid\text{No}) &= \frac{2.667}{3.667} \approx 0.727
\end{align*}
\boxed{P(\text{Yes})\approx0.476,\ P(\text{No})\approx0.524,\ P(\text{Rainy}\mid\text{Yes})=0.4,\ P(\text{Rainy}\mid\text{No})\approx0.727}
These updated parameters would now be used to redo the E-step, and the cycle repeats until the parameters stop changing (convergence).

Reinforcement Learning (RL) is a learning setting in which an \textbf{agent} interacts with an \textbf{environment} over a sequence of time steps. At each step the agent observes the current \textbf{state}, chooses an \textbf{action}, and receives a numerical \textbf{reward} along with the next state. The agent learns, purely through this trial-and-error interaction, a \textbf{policy} (a rule for choosing actions) that maximizes the total reward it collects over time.

Unlike every algorithm in Chapters 2 and 3, RL has no fixed dataset at all — the agent generates its own training data by acting and observing the consequences.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={rectangle,rounded corners,draw=primaryblue,thick,fill=primaryblue!8,align=center,font=\small,minimum width=2.6cm,minimum height=1cm},
]
\node (agent) at (0,0) {Agent};
\node (env) at (6,0) {Environment};
\draw[-{Stealth[length=2.5mm]},thick] (agent.north) to[bend left=25] node[above,font=\scriptsize]{action $a_t$} (env.north);
\draw[-{Stealth[length=2.5mm]},thick] (env.south) to[bend left=25] node[below,font=\scriptsize]{state $s_{t+1}$ and reward $r_{t+1}$} (agent.south);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

An agent receives rewards $4,3,2$ over three consecutive steps. Compute $G_0$ using $\gamma=0.9$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
G_0 &= 4 + (0.9)(3) + (0.9)^2(2) \\
&= 4 + 2.7 + (0.81)(2) \\
&= 4 + 2.7 + 1.62
\end{align*}
\boxed{G_0 = 8.32}

Using the same rewards as Example 4.7 ($4,3,2$), compute $G_0$ using $\gamma=0.5$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
G_0 &= 4 + (0.5)(3) + (0.5)^2(2) \\
&= 4 + 1.5 + (0.25)(2) \\
&= 4 + 1.5 + 0.5
\end{align*}
\boxed{G_0 = 6}
Comparing Examples 4.7 and 4.8: the closer $\gamma$ is to $1$, the more the later rewards ($3$ and $2$) count toward the total; the closer $\gamma$ is to $0$, the more the agent behaves as if only the immediate reward mattered.

An agent receives rewards $-1,-1,10$ over three steps (two small penalties followed by a large reward). Compute $G_0$ using $\gamma=0.9$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
G_0 &= -1 + (0.9)(-1) + (0.9)^2(10) \\
&= -1 - 0.9 + (0.81)(10) \\
&= -1 - 0.9 + 8.1
\end{align*}
\boxed{G_0 = 6.2}
Even though the immediate reward is negative, the large discounted future reward makes the overall return positive — this is exactly why an RL agent must plan for delayed consequences rather than just the next single reward.

\textbf{Reading the Discount Factor $\gamma$:}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item $\gamma$ close to $0$: the agent is short-sighted and cares mainly about the immediate reward
\item $\gamma$ close to $1$: the agent is far-sighted and gives future rewards nearly as much weight as immediate ones
\item $\gamma$ must satisfy $0\le\gamma<1$ so that the infinite sum $G_t$ stays finite for an ongoing task
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Markov Chains}

The Markov property states that the future state depends only on the present state, not on the sequence of states that preceded it:
\[
P(s_{t+1} \mid s_t, s_{t-1}, \ldots, s_1) = P(s_{t+1} \mid s_t)
\]
This is what makes the system "memoryless" beyond the current state.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[->,>=stealth,shorten >=1pt,thick,node distance=4cm,
  state/.style={circle,draw=primaryblue,very thick,fill=primaryblue!8,minimum size=1.5cm,font=\small}]
\node[state] (S) {Sunny};
\node[state] (R) [right of=S] {Rainy};
\path (S) edge [loop above] node[font=\scriptsize]{0.8} (S)
      (S) edge [bend left,above] node[font=\scriptsize]{0.2} (R)
      (R) edge [loop above] node[font=\scriptsize]{0.6} (R)
      (R) edge [bend left,below] node[font=\scriptsize]{0.4} (S);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item Every transition probability satisfies $0 \le P(j\mid i) \le 1$ \hfill\textit{(valid probability)}
\item The probabilities leaving any single state must sum to exactly $1$ \hfill\textit{(row sums to 1)}
\item The probability of an entire path is the \textbf{product} of the transition probabilities along that path \hfill\textit{(chain rule under the Markov property)}
\end{enumerate}

Using the transition probabilities in the diagram above (Sunny$\to$Sunny $0.8$, Sunny$\to$Rainy $0.2$, Rainy$\to$Rainy $0.6$, Rainy$\to$Sunny $0.4$), find the probability of the sequence Sunny, Sunny, Rainy given that day 1 is known to be Sunny.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
P(\text{Sunny},\text{Sunny},\text{Rainy}) &= P(S_1=\text{Sunny}) \cdot P(\text{Sunny}\to\text{Sunny}) \cdot P(\text{Sunny}\to\text{Rainy}) \\
&= 1 \times 0.8 \times 0.2
\end{align*}
\boxed{P = 0.16}

Using the same transition probabilities, find the probability of the sequence Rainy, Rainy, Sunny, Sunny given that day 1 is known to be Rainy.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
P(\text{Rainy},\text{Rainy},\text{Sunny},\text{Sunny}) &= 1 \times P(\text{Rainy}\to\text{Rainy}) \times P(\text{Rainy}\to\text{Sunny}) \times P(\text{Sunny}\to\text{Sunny}) \\
&= 1 \times 0.6 \times 0.4 \times 0.8
\end{align*}
\boxed{P = 0.192}

Using the same transition probabilities, find the probability that day 3 is Sunny given that day 1 is known to be Sunny (day 2 can be either state).

\textbf{Solution:}
Since day 2 is unknown, sum over both possibilities for day 2 (law of total probability):
\begin{align*}
P(S_3=\text{Sunny}\mid S_1=\text{Sunny}) &= P(S\to S)P(S\to S) + P(S\to R)P(R\to S) \\
&= (0.8)(0.8) + (0.2)(0.4) \\
&= 0.64 + 0.08
\end{align*}
\boxed{P(S_3=\text{Sunny}\mid S_1=\text{Sunny}) = 0.72}
Notice the difference from Example 4.10: here the in-between state is \textbf{not} fixed, so every possible path to the destination must be added together, rather than following one single specified path.

\textbf{Single-Path vs Marginal Probability:}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item If every intermediate state in the sequence is specified, simply \textbf{multiply} the transition probabilities along that one path (Examples 4.10, 4.11)
\item If an intermediate state is \textbf{not} specified, \textbf{sum} the products over every possible value it could take (Example 4.12)
\item This is the same "multiply along a branch, add across branches" rule used in probability trees
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Hidden Markov Models}

A Hidden Markov Model extends a Markov chain to situations where the true states are \textbf{not directly observable}. Instead, at each time step the hidden state probabilistically produces a visible \textbf{observation}. An HMM is defined by three components: the \textbf{transition probabilities} $A$ between hidden states, the \textbf{emission probabilities} $B$ of each hidden state producing each observation, and the \textbf{initial probabilities} $\pi$ of starting in each hidden state.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[->,>=stealth,shorten >=1pt,thick,node distance=4cm,
  state/.style={circle,draw=primaryblue,very thick,fill=primaryblue!8,minimum size=1.5cm,font=\small},
  obs/.style={rectangle,draw=accentorange,very thick,fill=accentorange!10,minimum size=1cm,font=\small}]
\node[state] (S) {Sunny};
\node[state] (R) [right of=S] {Rainy};
\node[obs] (OS) [below of=S,node distance=2.2cm] {Walk};
\node[obs] (OR) [below of=R,node distance=2.2cm] {Clean};
\path (S) edge [loop above] node[font=\scriptsize]{0.7} (S)
      (S) edge [bend left,above] node[font=\scriptsize]{0.3} (R)
      (R) edge [loop above] node[font=\scriptsize]{0.6} (R)
      (R) edge [bend left,below] node[font=\scriptsize]{0.4} (S);
\draw[accentorange,dashed,thick,->] (S) -- node[left,font=\scriptsize]{0.6} (OS);
\draw[accentorange,dashed,thick,->] (R) -- node[right,font=\scriptsize]{0.5} (OR);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}
\begin{center}
{\small Solid arrows: hidden state transitions ($A$). Dashed arrows: emission probabilities ($B$) to observations.}
\end{center}

An HMM has $\pi(\text{Sunny})=0.6$, $\pi(\text{Rainy})=0.4$. Transitions: Sunny$\to$Sunny $0.7$, Sunny$\to$Rainy $0.3$, Rainy$\to$Rainy $0.6$, Rainy$\to$Sunny $0.4$. Emissions: $P(\text{Walk}\mid\text{Sunny})=0.6$, $P(\text{Shop}\mid\text{Sunny})=0.3$, $P(\text{Walk}\mid\text{Rainy})=0.1$, $P(\text{Shop}\mid\text{Rainy})=0.4$.
Compute $P(\text{states}=\text{Sunny},\text{Sunny};\ \text{obs}=\text{Walk},\text{Shop})$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
P &= \pi(\text{Sunny}) \cdot P(\text{Walk}\mid\text{Sunny}) \cdot P(\text{Sunny}\to\text{Sunny}) \cdot P(\text{Shop}\mid\text{Sunny}) \\
&= (0.6)(0.6) \times (0.7)(0.3) \\
&= 0.36 \times 0.21
\end{align*}
\boxed{P = 0.0756}

Using the same HMM as Example 4.13, compute $P(\text{states}=\text{Sunny},\text{Rainy};\ \text{obs}=\text{Walk},\text{Clean})$ where $P(\text{Clean}\mid\text{Rainy})=0.5$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
P &= \pi(\text{Sunny}) \cdot P(\text{Walk}\mid\text{Sunny}) \cdot P(\text{Sunny}\to\text{Rainy}) \cdot P(\text{Clean}\mid\text{Rainy}) \\
&= (0.6)(0.6) \times (0.3)(0.5) \\
&= 0.36 \times 0.15
\end{align*}
\boxed{P = 0.054}

Using the same HMM as Examples 4.13 and 4.14, compute \\ 
$P(\text{states}=\text{Rainy},\text{Rainy},\text{Sunny};\ \text{obs}=\text{Shop},\text{Clean},\text{Walk})$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
P &= \pi(\text{Rainy}) \cdot P(\text{Shop}\mid\text{Rainy}) \cdot P(\text{Rainy}\to\text{Rainy}) \cdot P(\text{Clean}\mid\text{Rainy}) \cdot P(\text{Rainy}\to\text{Sunny})\\ \cdot P(\text{Walk}\mid\text{Sunny}) \\
&= (0.4)(0.4) \times (0.6)(0.5) \times (0.4)(0.6) \\
&= 0.16 \times 0.3 \times 0.24
\end{align*}
\boxed{P = 0.01152}

\textbf{Markov Chain vs Hidden Markov Model:}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Markov Chain} & \textbf{Hidden Markov Model} \\
\midrule
States & Directly observed & Hidden (not observed) \\
What we see & The state sequence itself & Only an observation sequence \\
Parameters needed & Transition probabilities & Transition \textit{and} emission probabilities \\
Typical question & Probability of a state path & Probability of an observation sequence \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\newpage

A policy $\pi$ is a rule that specifies which action the agent takes in each state, written $\pi(s)=a$ for a deterministic policy. Solving an MDP means finding the policy that maximizes the expected discounted return from every state.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[->,>=stealth,shorten >=1pt,thick,node distance=3.6cm,
  state/.style={circle,draw=primaryblue,very thick,fill=primaryblue!8,minimum size=1.5cm,font=\small}]
\node[state] (S1) {$S_1$};
\node[state] (S2) [right of=S1] {$S_2$};
\node[state] (S3) [right of=S2] {$S_3$ (goal)};
\path (S1) edge node[above,font=\scriptsize]{$a_1$, $r=-1$} (S2)
      (S2) edge node[above,font=\scriptsize]{$a_1$, $r=+10$} (S3)
      (S1) edge [loop above] node[font=\scriptsize]{$a_2$, $r=0$} (S1);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\subsection{Value Functions and the Bellman Equation}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item A \textbf{goal / terminal state} has value $V(s)=0$, since no further reward is collected after reaching it \hfill\textit{(base case)}
\item Values can be computed \textbf{backward} from the goal state toward earlier states \hfill\textit{(backward induction)}
\item The \textbf{optimal} policy $\pi^\ast$ at each state chooses the action leading to the highest $R(s,a,s')+\gamma V(s')$ \hfill\textit{(greedy w.r.t.\ the value function)}
\end{enumerate}

Using the diagram above ($S_1 \xrightarrow{a_1,r=-1} S_2 \xrightarrow{a_1,r=+10} S_3$, with $S_3$ terminal), compute $V(S_3)$, $V(S_2)$, and $V(S_1)$ using $\gamma=0.9$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
V(S_3) &= 0 \quad \text{(terminal state)} \\[4pt]
V(S_2) &= R(S_2,a_1,S_3) + \gamma V(S_3) = 10 + (0.9)(0) = 10 \\[4pt]
V(S_1) &= R(S_1,a_1,S_2) + \gamma V(S_2) = -1 + (0.9)(10) = -1+9
\end{align*}
\boxed{V(S_3)=0,\quad V(S_2)=10,\quad V(S_1)=8}

In state $S_1$ from Example 4.22, action $a_1$ leads to $S_2$ (reward $-1$) and action $a_2$ leads back to $S_1$ itself (reward $0$, i.e.\ staying put, so its "next value" is $V(S_1)$ again). Using $V(S_2)=10$ from Example 4.22 and $\gamma=0.9$, which action should the optimal policy choose at $S_1$: $a_1$ or $a_2$?

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
\text{Value of taking } a_1 &= R(S_1,a_1,S_2)+\gamma V(S_2) = -1+(0.9)(10) = 8 \\
\text{Value of taking } a_2 &= R(S_1,a_2,S_1)+\gamma V(S_1) = 0 + (0.9)(0) = 0 \quad (\text{using an initial guess } V(S_1)=0)
\end{align*}
Since $8 > 0$:
\boxed{\text{Optimal action at } S_1 \text{ is } a_1 \text{ (move toward the goal, not stay in place)}}
This matches the general rule from the theorembox: the optimal policy always picks the action with the higher $R(s,a,s')+\gamma V(s')$. \checkmark

A chain has $S_1 \xrightarrow{r=2} S_2 \xrightarrow{r=3} S_3 \xrightarrow{r=5} S_4$(terminal). Compute $V(S_4)$, $V(S_3)$, $V(S_2)$, $V(S_1)$ using $\gamma=0.5$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
V(S_4) &= 0 \\
V(S_3) &= 5+(0.5)(0) = 5 \\
V(S_2) &= 3+(0.5)(5) = 3+2.5 = 5.5 \\
V(S_1) &= 2+(0.5)(5.5) = 2+2.75
\end{align*}
\boxed{V(S_4)=0,\ V(S_3)=5,\ V(S_2)=5.5,\ V(S_1)=4.75}
